#!/usr/bin/env sh
# peaks-loop push gate. Design + why-it-is-a-ratchet: .husky/peaks-gate.mjs
#
# Both legs are scoped to WHAT YOU ARE PUSHING (slice B5, 2026-09-23). Nothing
# here looks at the whole repo any more.
#
#   1. `changed` mode — the SAME per-file ratchet as the commit gate, applied to
#      `git diff --name-only origin/main...HEAD` instead of the index. A file you
#      touched may not be worse than its own line in
#      .peaks/lint/gate-baseline.json; a file the diff ADDS must be clean; a file
#      the diff DELETES contributes 0 and is not handed to eslint.
#
#   2. `pnpm test:changed` — the repo's EXISTING affected-tests mechanism
#      (scripts/test-changed.mjs, wired since slice 2 as `test:changed`). Not a
#      new script: it classifies the same diff and runs the tests that diff
#      implies, falling back to the FULL unit suite for a change it declines to
#      reason about (package.json / pnpm-lock.yaml / vitest.config.ts /
#      tsconfig.json / scripts/ / .claude/ / .peaks/, or an empty diff).
#
#      KNOW WHAT THAT MEANS IN PRACTICE. The paragraph above USED to end here
#      with "`.peaks/` is a trigger and every slice regenerates the baseline, so
#      leg 2 IS the full suite" — measured 62s/601 tests on 2026-10-09, that is
#      no longer true, and the reason is in the code: `fullFallbackExempt` in
#      scripts/test-changed.mjs exempts `.peaks/lint/gate-baseline.json` from the
#      trigger list. Re-read it before trusting a number in this comment.
#
#      What it is today, measured on this host 2026-10-09:
#        - a baseline + `src/cli/commands/**` diff  -> 71 files / 601 tests,  62s
#        - a diff that also touches package.json    ->425 files /4422 tests, 304s
#        - a diff touching ONLY the baseline        ->  0 tests (by design; the
#          exemption says so and the script prints it)
#      So the hook is a subset OR the full suite depending on the diff, and the
#      old "936s, a known measured gap" no longer describes it. Do not skip it
#      with --no-verify on the strength of a stale number.
#
#      The consequence worth knowing: a change that touches only the baseline
#      runs ZERO tests here, so the gate is not what proves such a change.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A WEAKER GATE THAN THE WHOLE-REPO ONE IT REPLACED
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The baseline stores a number PER FILE, so the per-file check is not a proxy
# for the total check — it is the total check, decomposed:
#
#     every changed file ≤ its own baseline        (leg 1, per file)
#     AND every new file contributes 0             (leg 1, per file)
#     AND every deleted file contributes 0         (leg 1 drops it entirely)
#   ⟹ total = Σ(untouched files' baselines) + Σ(changed files' actuals) + 0
#            ≤ Σ(all baselines) ≤ the whole-repo ceiling.
#
# Measured against the 2026-09-22 baseline: Σ(per-file eslint baselines) = 2878,
# exactly the `eslintFindings` ceiling — an identity here, not an approximation.
# So if this file passes, the whole-repo totals it used to check CANNOT have
# grown upward. That is an entailment; the old check was the special case of it.
#
# What the per-file check cannot see: growth on a file NOBODY touched, caused by
# CONFIG DRIFT (a rule quietly switched off or on, a phantom rule appearing, a
# coverage hole opening). That class is rare, real, and is nobody's commit — so
# it is paid for by a machine instead of by a human waiting to push. CI owns it:
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `vitest + build` job, step "Whole-repo lint
# ratchet", runs `node .husky/peaks-gate.mjs repo`. That job already ran the full
# unit suite (`npx vitest run`), so the ratchet was the only missing piece — the
# two coexist and do not overlap (the ratchet contains no tests).
#
# WHY IT MOVED AT ALL: `repo` mode is ~9 type-aware eslint programs (each
# rebuilding the TS program) plus a whole-program `tsc`, and its cost is a
# function of the WHOLE REPO. The changed mode's cost is a function of the DIFF:
# measured on this host 2026-09-23, the whole-repo gate took 126s, the same gate
# over a 49-file slice took 7s. (The slice that requested this change recorded
# 8+ minutes for the repo gate; that number did NOT reproduce here — 126s on
# re-measurement — and this repo has been burned before by a number taken while
# other work ran on the same box. Neither figure is the point: the point is that
# the pre-push cost no longer grows with the size of the repository.) A push gate
# that costs minutes stops being a "check it before I push" and becomes a thing
# people skip with `--no-verify` — and a gate people skip is worse than no gate,
# because it is also believed.
set -e
node .husky/peaks-gate.mjs changed
pnpm test:changed -- origin/main
